A||N||17||polomeno||Got head changed and now i get a loud clicking noise??||||1||
Z||000000||polomeno||08-02-2001||05:33 PM||||Hey, i had the head on my engine changed, 4 cylinder , and now i get a pretty load clicking from top of engine, mechanic says that it's just the rockers a bit tight and it will go away and suggested that i get some engine treatment.. so i got this stuff at canadian tire called 'go motor" for like $40 that is suppost to help... is this a common thing that will go away? or do i need to have it checked out?<P>thanks<P>John<p>[This message has been edited by polomeno (edited 08-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000001||StuGood||08-02-2001||05:42 PM||||Rockers "a bit tight" doesn't sound right to me.  My first guess would be valves not properly lashed (i.e. - rockers "a bit loose").  If you remove oil fill cap from the valve cover while it's running (rag handy to keep oil from gettting all over), does it get louder?||||reg||5
Z||000002||polomeno||08-02-2001||05:44 PM||||oh ya.. i'm sure its' the rockers.. i'm just curious if it's gonna go away after being "worn in" like he says or if i got a different problem on my hands... this engine made no noise when it has the other head with blown gasket ( leaked alot but at least it was quiet )<P>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000003||Fast85GT||08-02-2001||05:56 PM||||What kind of a mechanic do you have? Has to wear in? You don't want them too tight - it is harsh on your cam.  Plus, it would not be clicking probably.  One of my rocker arms are loose and it makes a clicking.  I need to adjust the lash.  Some mechanis will tell you that you don't adjust them.  This is not true.  They are hydro.  They are to be adjusted to an extent.  If it is the front of the engine, you are lucky.  However, you can remove the upper plenum (V6) and access the back rockers w/o pulling the intake.<P>------------------<BR>Jeremy H<BR>Black 1985 Pontiac Fiero GT||||reg||4
Z||000004||polomeno||08-02-2001||06:05 PM||||it's a 4 cylinder, can i just adjust them myself ? what am i looking for and how do i do it?<P>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000005||EdsB52||08-02-2001||06:15 PM||||The previous guy thought you had a 2.8 6cyl and was essentially directing you to follow the manual which tells you to rotate the engine (crankshaft) to a certain position, adjust various intake and exhaust valves, then rotate again and tighten more rockers. It's kind of an exhaustive process, but works well. Don't quote me on this, but I believe the 4 banger only requires you to tighten the rocker nuts down. It's been a while since I rebuilt my 2.5's, but I believe they just get tightened down. If that's the case and they're not tight, then you simply have loose rockers. Check the manual before you do it. When I rebuilt my 2.5's they ran beautifully and never made unusual noises and I had one of them for 75,000 miles without ever adjusting the valve lash.||||reg||1
Z||000006||polomeno||08-02-2001||06:18 PM||||cool, thanks for the help, does anyone know for sure how to adjust these, or has done it lately?<P>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000007||DecadenceR||08-02-2001||09:42 PM||||When I rebuilt the top of my 4 and the rockers were too tight, the motor didn't click, it ran like **** . Almost like I was missing a plug. If you want to check to see if it's too tight, the bolt should only be torqued to 24 ft/lbs. Anything more than that is too tight.<P>I have a clicking sound. It turned out that it's my exhaust manifold. I made it tighter and it pretty much went away. Maybe you should check that out.||||reg||1
Z||000008||rogergarrison||08-02-2001||10:16 PM||||loose rocker would make it louder than a tite one. could just have a collapsed lifter. may pump itself back up. are you sure you got the pushrods seated on the lifters?||||reg||1
Z||000009||MrPBody||08-02-2001||10:46 PM||||What year is your car?<P>I have read a few recent posts about valve noise after rebuild, particularly with '87-'88 4-cylinder engine.  Ogre hasn't figured it out yet, but he's working on it.<P>I recently replaced the head on my '85 4-cylinder.  It had no way to adjust valve lash (like V-6 does.)  Just tighten the rockers solid, and it all magically works right <B>IF</B> the valve stems are the correct height, and the pushrods are the correct length, and and the head hasn't been milled too much, and the wind is from the northwest, and the moon is full, and you sacrifice a red chicken.<P>I don't think motor goo will help.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||DaveL||08-03-2001||08:59 AM||||According to the Haynes manual, you just tighten them to the specified torque, and drive. If they are noisy after that you will already be tightened to your own specified torque.....||||reg||1
Z||000011||polomeno||08-03-2001||01:39 PM||||My car is an 84, 4 speed, i will check the book and torque them to 24 lbs or whichever it is and see if it works.. i'll let you guys know<P>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000012||Fast85GT||08-03-2001||03:16 PM||||I'm no expert on the 4, but if it has mechanical lifters, (non-hydro), there should be a specified valve lash gap.  You use a feeler gauge and check the gap.  It seems to me to be a better way than torquing.<P>------------------<BR>Jeremy H<BR>Black 1985 Pontiac Fiero GT||||reg||1
Z||000013||Posthumane||08-03-2001||04:36 PM||||The four has hydro lifters. If they click after the rcockers have been properly tightened, than something is off in your valve geometry. If you overtighten them to the point where the hydros are fully compressed, then the valves won't close all the way and you get the 'running like ****' condition that decadencer mentioned.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Frank Sombers||08-04-2001||10:41 PM||||A RED CHICKEN!?! Here I was sacrificing<BR>goats! No wonder my Duke still makes a lot<BR>of noise.||||reg||10
Z||000015||polomeno||08-05-2001||12:45 PM||||i checked my book but i'm missing the whole first chapter where it talks about adjusting the 4 cylinder rockers.. but it does list the torque at 20 lbs.. is there anything else i need to do other then make sure the torque is correct??<P>thanks<P>John||||reg||1
Z||000016||DecadenceR||08-05-2001||08:26 PM||||Polo- Like I said, i did this a few months ago. I took off the valve cover and I ran the motor for a few seconds and I watched. This does get messy because oil splashes all over the fire wall, but it was needed at this point. <BR>Watch your pushrods to make shre you didn't bend one. If you did, you'll be able to tell with it running. watch all the rocker arms to make sure that all of them are moving the same. if any aren't moving as much as the other or are moving a lot more, then you need to check the torque on that one.<P>Turns out what i did wrong on mine was I used a different rocker arm bolt (different year, but the dealer said it was the right one), cause I snapped one. And that bolt was shorter than the others. So even though I had the right torque, it was still holding the valve open, which was also ruining the pushrod. But even after I fixed it, there was a ticking noise. I rigured out it was the exhaust manifold. I tigntened it the best I could, but you can still hear it.||||reg||1
Z||000017||EdsB52||08-05-2001||09:02 PM||||Nope, like I originally assumed, just tighten down. Torque shouldn't matter, because if they're over-torqued to the point of collapsing the lifters, you've either made new threads or have just snapped of the studs.||||reg||1
